  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.4. Increased capacity of member States and enterprises to develop policies and measures that promote the alignment of business practices with decent work and a human-centred approach to the future of work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) National Action Plan on Business and Human Rights  was approved by the  Ministry of Labour (MoL) through an internal Government process in Thailand. Subsequently, the MoL adopted and started to implement its respective actions. Though the NAP was adopted in 2019 the implementation started in 2020.
2) Tripartite constituents in Thailand formally established policy dialogue spaces such as the task force to promote socially responsible and sustainable business in auto parts supply chains, which has its action plan through a set of policy recommendations with concrete actions which have been the outcome of the first meeting of the task force in question held on 22 May, 2020. 
3) A similar action plan has been the outcome of the tripartite Consultative Meeting on the Establishment of a Technical Working Group on Promoting Socially Responsible and Sustainable Business in Agriculture Supply Chains held on 24 November 2021, which had its respective set of action recommendations included in the meeting report through the &quot;Action plan toward the establishment of a Technical Working Group on Promoting Socially Responsible and Sustainable Business in Agriculture Supply Chains.
 The ILO&apos;s business support services promoted the alignment of business practices (e.g. opportunities in upskilling and reskilling) with decent work priorities and human-centred approach to the future of work. The ILO provided &quot;In Business&quot; training to enhance cognitive skills of 14,183 women workers by 2021, skills that are critical to enhance productivity and career advancement. 
ECOT initiated dialogue with the Ministry of Labour for In Business to be acknowledged in the Skills Development Promotion Act. The &quot;Ready for Business&quot; is a similar business support services catered for youth and it reached 614 youth in 2021. Constituents have raised awareness, developed knowledge and capacities on the implementation of socially responsible labour practices aligned to international instruments on RBC/CSR (MNE Declaration, OECD guidelines for MNEs and UNGPs) in order to promote and implement International Labour Standards and decent working conditions at the enterprise level. Particular focus was on the automotive and agricultural sectors and to be replicated broadly through national policies and actions. 
The ILO has supported the establishment of policy dialogue spaces such as the task force to promote socially responsible and sustainable business in auto parts supply chains and in agriculture sector. The dialogues, policy discussions and workshops covering MNEs (buyers) and SMEs (suppliers) have been informed through action-research in both sectors, analyzing the respective supply chains. The ILO worked with tripartite constituents to increase the knowledge, capacities and awareness of their members on the relevance of socially responsible business practices as a way to mainstream ILS and FPRW, making more competitive and productive companies in the targeted sectors by the RCSA programme. These trainings were replicated to enterprises in the supply chains of the poultry sector, to labour inspectors, SMEs in the auto-parts sector and workers members of NCPE and TTUC.</narrative>
   </description>
